sections.data set:
The study population consisted of patients of all ages from the IPCI database who consulted their GP in the period from 2017 to 2023. Follow-up began once patients had been registered for at least one year, but no earlier than January 1st, 2017. Follow-up was terminated at the earliest occurrence of one of the following events: patient death, transfer to another GP, or December 31st, 2023. A consultation with a GP was defined as a registered patient contact with a GP, where the documented data are assigned to a new or existing ICPC code. For consultations not assigned to ICPC-code, ‘care service code,’ and/or ‘integrated care code were used. ‘Care service codes’ are used in GP information systems to indicate the type and/or the duration of the consultation. An ‘integrated care code’ is used to indicate that a patient is receiving integrated care. Integrated care refers to coordinated approach to healthcare where various services and providers work together to deliver comprehensive and seamless care, especially for patients with chronic health conditions. In case multiple new or existing ICPC-codes were assigned during a single contact with a GP, these were interpreted as multiple consultations. Over the period 2017–2023, we determined the total number of consultations and the proportion attributed to each ICPC code, categorized by chapter (broad categories of health problems based on body systems or thematic groups) and by calendar year. L Chapter codes represent musculoskeletal consultations, which were compared with those in the other chapters. The proportion for MSCs in Dutch general practice was shown as a percentage of the total number of consultations at the general practice per calendar year. The 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for the proportion for MSCs were calculated using the normal approximation formula \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
