sections.a critical mitochondrial adaptation faci:
To investigate the impact of temperature on the global melting profile of mitochondrial-encoded RNAs (meRNAs), we applied the intercalating dye Sybrgreen I, which separates from double-stranded regions of meRNAs upon denaturation of the transcript. As a complementary method, the metachromatic property of acridine orange was utilised. Mitochondria were isolated from NPCs and total mitochondrial RNAs were extracted and dissolved in RNase-free 18mΩ water. The RNA binding dyes were then added, and an optical reader was used to record the fluorescence signal. Both methodologies reported a similar melting profile with a population-level average Tm of 51 °C for meRNAs at a concentration of 2 µg in 50 µl of RNase-free 18mΩ water (Fig. 5 A, left). To complement this finding, we studied the Tm of select meRNAs using a more accurate methodology. We initially used the UNAfold platform to identify regions within mouse meRNAs that can form a double-stranded helix and to estimate the ΔG of these helices (Fig. 5 A, middle). From the thermodynamic profile of helices, tRNAs appeared to form more extensive and stable helices. Two tRNAs were selected, tRNA-Ala and tRNA-Arg, with a ΔΔG of 5 kcal/mol. The synthetic tRNAs (1 µg in 50 µl of RNase-free 18mΩ water) were labelled with a fluorescence reporter, 5′-FAM, and a quencher, 3′-TAMRA, to study the denaturation kinetics of the tRNA. In a control experiment, mixed meRNAs were added to the synthetic tRNAs anticipating that in a mixed population helices with a low ΔG will absorb the thermal energy and will hence function as a thermal buffer delaying the denaturation of tRNAs (Fig. 5 A, right). While tRNA-Ala showed a T m of 53 °C in a mixed pool of meRNAs, tRNA-Arg had a T m of 38 °C. The measured ΔT m of 15 °C was consistent with the calculated ΔΔG of 5 kcal/mol as for a typical 10–20 bp RNA duplex. Total ΔS is expected to be within a range of − 250 to − 350 cal/mol/K [ 58 ] and near Tm of the duplex: \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
