				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:\varDelta\:H={T}_{m}.\varDelta\:S\:\:\to\:\:\:\:{T}_{m}=\:\frac{\varDelta\:H}{\varDelta\:S}=\frac{5000}{300}\approx\:17$$\end{document} Hence, the thermal output of mitochondria triggered by IOC (Fig. 4 I) is within an optimal range (38 °C to 53 °C) to eliminate the secondary structure of a majority of the mouse meRNAs with melting propensities within the Tm range measured for tRNA-Arg and tRNA-Ala. While denaturation of meRNAs facilitates hybridisation to mitochondrial ssDNA, the competing single stranded binding protein 1 (Ssbp1 is expected to associate with mitochondrial ssDNA and prevent binding of meRNAs. Hybridisation of meRNAs to mitochondrial ssDNA suggests that either the protein is not present in sufficient quantities or that binding of Ssbp1 to ssDNA is somewhat compromised during early neuronal differentiation. Fig. 5 RNA/DNA duplex formation is supported by a structural adaptation of mitochondrial Ssbp1. (A) Left plot sows global melting profile of meRNAs isolated from NPCs. Bar plot shows the distribution of ΔG of duplex regions of meRNAs. Right diagrams show melting profiles of tRNA-Ala and tRNA-Arg, in isolation or with pooled meRNAs, recorded using FRET. (B) Thermal stability shift profile of recombinant mitochondrial proteins. (C) Diagram shows the RSMF plot of mouse Ssbp1 at 37 °C and 50 °C. (D) Plot shows thermal stability shift profile of recombinant E. coli Ssb. Bottom plot shows RMSF profile of E. coli Ssb at 37 °C and 50 °C. (E) RMSF plots of mouse Ssbp1 simulated at 50 °C after in silico mutation of P92 > L92. Gel shows DNA profile of mitochondria isolated from control NPCs or those expressing E. coli Ssb or a mutated mouse Ssbp1 (P92 > L92). (F) RT-qPCR analysis of meRNAs isolated from control NPCs or those expressing E. coli Ssb or a mutated mouse Ssbp1 (P92 > L92). Data are presented as normalised values to the baseline in each group. * two-tailed p-value < 0.001, n = 3 biological replicates. (G) Analysis of the abundance of mDNA at different timepoints upon induction of differentiation in control NPCs or those expressing E. coli Ssb or a mutated mouse Ssbp1 (P92 > L92). n = 3 biological replicates Analysis of proteomic data revealed that the level of Ssbp1 increased from T0 to 0.5 h post-induction of differentiation (supplementary Data S3). It was therefore hypothesized that binding of Ssbp1 to ssDNA could potentially be compromised during early neuronal differentiation. A possibility that thermal flux affects binding of Ssbp1 to ssDNA was probed using a thermal shift assay, with Sypro orange as the reporter, and employing recombinant mouse Ssbp1 protein alone or in association with a circular ssDNA (corresponding to OriL flanked by the upstream region (-40 bp: C5119-G5158) and the downstream region (+ 45 bp: A5196-T5242) of the OriL). In parallel control experiments, we used recombinant mouse DNA polymerase gamma (Pol-γ), mitochondrial RNA polymerase (Polmrt) alone, and Polmrt bound to the mitochondrial origin of replication of light strand (OriL). The recombinant Polmrt and Polmrt/OriL were not destabilised as a function of increased temperature (Fig. 5 B). Likewise, the stability of recombinant Pol-γ did not appear to be affected by an increasing temperature. This is evidence by an absence of significant increase in Sypro orange signal intensity up to 48 °C, indicating that the hydrophobic residues of recombinant Pol-γ remain buried in the interior (core) of the folded protein. The recombinant Ssbp1, on the other hand, became destabilised as a function of temperature with the peak of instability occurring at 49 °C (Fig. 5 B). This surprising finding prompted search for a molecular basis for the thermal instability of Ssbp1. To find a clue, we simulated molecular dynamics of Ssbp1 at 37 °C and 50 °C using Gromacs package. A model of mouse Ssbp1 (AF- Q9CYR0 -F1-v4) was used in the analysis. Figure 5 C shows the RMSF (root mean square fluctuation), which correlates the fluctuation, per amino acid during the trajectory of the dynamics, for (α)-carbon of amino acids. A region corresponding to W 84 -M 119 of Ssbp1 fluctuated significantly more than other regions of the protein at 50 °C (Fig. 5 C). Notably, the stacking energy of W 84 contributes to the binding of Ssbp to ssDNA [ 59 ] and fluctuations of this region could potentially compromise the association of the protein and DNA. To understand the molecular basis for thermal instability of mouse Ssbp1, we mapped and compared adaptations that underpin a divergent evolution of the protein across the prokaryotic and eukaryotic domains of life. Studying E. coli single-stranded DNA-binding protein (Ssb) using thermal shift assay disclosed remarkable thermal stability of the protein at 50 °C, a behaviour that clearly contrasted with thermal instability of mouse Ssbp1 (Fig. 5 D, top). Corroborating the experimental results, MD analysis revealed that temperature-dependent fluctuation of (α)-carbons of E. coli Ssb at 37 °C and 50 °C were within the same atomic range (Fig. 5 D, bottom). Local alignment of the region corresponding to W 84 -M 119 of mouse Ssbp1 to E. coli Ssb indicated that the region was highly conserved both in prokaryotic and eukaryotic domains of life (Fig. 5 D), an observation that is not surprising given the reported role of this region in binding to single-stranded DNA [ 59 ]. Interestingly, P 92 appeared to be a structural signature of the mitochondria protein that is absent in all the other studied prokaryotes (supplementary Fig. S9). This highly conserved proline preceded an alpha helix “(G 93 -Y 101 ), an observation that was at odds with the capacity of the amino acid to function as a disruptor of helices [ 60 ], unless it was conserved to operate as a thermal destabiliser. We tested whether P 92 could potentially contribute to the thermal instability of W 84 -M 119 . A conservative in silico mutation of proline to leucine (P 92 > L 92 ) followed by molecular dynamics analysis provided theoretical validation that P 92 is a major contributor to the thermal instability of mouse Ssbp1 (Fig. 5 E, left, supplementary Fig. S9). Consistent with these findings, uploading mouse mitochondria with Ssbp P92> L92 or E. coli Ssb after transfection of NPCs using a eukaryotic expression vector followed by induction of neuronal differentiation, we observed lower abundance of RNA/DNA duplex ( Fig. 5E , right). Also, the level of meRNAs in NPCs expressing Ssbp P92> L92 or E. coli Ssb did not decrease upon induction of neuronal differentiation providing further assurance that ssDNA operates as a natural antisense DNA triggering RNase H-mediated degradation of meRNAs ( Fig. 5F ). This led us to question if offsetting this mitochondrial adaptation (thermally unstable Ssbp1) would be sufficient to prevent mitochondrial decline during neuronal differentiation. Quantifying the level of mtDNA with primers that hybridised to Nd5 locus provided evidence that mitochondria remain completely viable in NPCs expressing E. coli Ssb, while Ssbp P92> L92 partially prevents mitochondrial decline ( Fig. 5G ). We concluded that mitochondrial thermogenesis not only triggers partial denaturation of meRNAs but it also destabilises Ssbp1 providing an opportunity for formation of RNA/DNA duplexes. We next asked what uncouples replication of mitochondrial light and heavy strands leading to generation of single-stranded H-strand.

