sections.translational pkpd:
First, relevant PK and efficacy and toxicity data were extracted from preclinical models (for details see supplementary material). Studies in VWM mice showed that a 4.5 mg/kg daily dose resulted in a better clinical efficacy than a 10 mg/kg weekly dose, suggesting that the total area under the curve (AUC) is the driver of clinical efficacy rather than the C max 13 . Therefore, we translated the possible effective dose on AUC from mice to children. Previous studies in rats showed accumulation and some delayed absorption of guanabenz in the brain 15 . To scale optimal dose from brain AUC from rodents to children, plasma PK parameters from mice, brain ratio’s from rats and adult plasma PK data 15 – 17 were combined to infer the PK profile and accumulation of guanabenz in the brain in children at the optimal predicted dose level (figure S1 ). In short, and explained in more detail in the supplementary materials, linear compartmental models were used to approximate the observed mouse and rat PK-data in plasma and brain 15 , 17 . The observed data in mice and rat were adequately estimated using two-compartment models, with equilibrium force function with two parameters, the rate of distribution (KPT) and ratio between plasma and the brain (RPT) from plasma into the brain, to describe the delayed transfer of Guanabenz from rat-plasma to brain (Supplemental 1.1.1, Eq. 8 ). For plasma-PK, allometric scaling was used (equations in supplementary materials) among rodents, adults and children. Two methods were used to predict brain concentrations in children. In the first, the rate and extent of penetration from plasma into the brain (KPT & RPT) were directly translated from the rat model, without adjustment for differences in body composition 15 . In the second, differences in brain mass ratio between mice and humans were taken into account to correct both parameters (Table 1 ), as recent research showed that the permeability surface area product (PS) in ml/min/g tissue is well conserved among species 18 . Table 1 Parameters for plasma pharmacokinetics and brain tissue penetration. Parameters Mice a Rat b Extrapolated from Rats Extrapolated from adults c Final model (RSE)[shrinkage] (RSE)[shrinkage] (RSE)[shrinkage] Weight (kg) 0.03 0.25 15 15 15 Cl (L/h/kg); IIV 7.67 a (2) 4.56 b (6) 1.6 16.2 7 (20.9) Vd (L/kg); IIV 8.83 a (6) 4.4 b (19) 4.4 71 54.9 (19.9) V2 (L/kg) 8.80 a (7) 3.87 b (22) 3.36 Q (L/h/kg) 10.53 a (15) 3.36 b (63) 1.20 KA (h-1); IIV 1.3 c 1.3 c 1.16 (39.7) KPT (rate L/h) 2.98 d 1.43 b (9) 14.1 d 14.1 d 14.1 d RPT (ratio) 29.6 d 14.2 b (2) 140.03 d 140.03 d 140.03 d BMR 0.015 0.0072 0.071 0.071 0.071 Absorption lag time (h) 0.39 WT on CL 0.75 0.75 0.764 (16) WT on Vd 1 1 1.02 (12) CV% CL 187 (101)[56] 93 (26) [58] 38.2 (23 )[9] CV% Vd 230 (207)[67] 50.3 (26) [19] 43.8 (24) [11] CV% K a 207 (62)[60] 78.9 (25) [31] 78.7 (39) [30] CV% Q 306 (6)[0.1] CV% V2 459 (12)[9.5] CV% prop. error. venous 5 (50.2) [0.1] 22 (4) [0.1] 37 (0.2)[20] 34 (13) [6] 32 (14) [6] CV% prop. error brain 73 (16) [0.1] CV% error DBS 58 (0.2)[19.4] 51 (14) [5] 48 (23) [5] Prediction error 0.48% −1.76% −100.0% −50.0% −4.5% CL : clearance; Vd : volume of distribution; V2 : volume of distribution of the second compartment; Q : intercompartmental clearance; KA : rate of absorption; KPT : rate of distribution from plasma to the brain; RPT : ratio of the concentration in plasma and brain compartment; BW : body weight; BMR : Ratio between brain and body weight; IIV; Interindividual variability (variability in PK was assessed in human PK studies only); RSE: relative standard error. CV%: Coefficient of variation. Errors are given in both proportional (prop.), and additive (add.). CL and V were allometry scaled using weight with the allometric scaling factor: (CL ~ WT) and (V ~ WT), respectively. a: Estimated with NONMEM using data from reference 17 b: Estimated with NONMEM using data from reference 15 . C: extrapolated with allometric scaling from reference 19 d: