sections.study design overview:
This modeling study developed an updated model utilizing nationally published data from Saudi Arabia, commencing in 2010. The study employs a probabilistic approach, the study leverages data to define statistical distributions and integrate them into the model's computational methodology. This approach is intended to improve the model’s capacity to manage uncertainty across the food system, from supply to consumption. It effectively captures weekly fluctuations in food consumption and accounts for age-related differences in intake [ 28 ]. These elements were not addressed in the initial version, which converted food supply to consumption without adjustments for age or weekly variations [ 20 ]. The computational modeling approach in this study is primarily based on the supply level (S), which is first converted into an unadjusted consumption estimate (F) using a supply-to-consumption coefficient derived from empirical data. This representation reflects a simplified schematic of the modeling framework. A more detailed formulation, including the full set of computational steps and associated parameters, is provided in Fig. 1 . Table 1 details the computational steps for these estimations and assessments, with input parameters provided in Appendix 1. Fig. 1 Simplified schematic of the computational modeling process Table 1 Data and parameters for modeling the food supply, consumption, and nutrient exposure in Saudi Arabia Variable Description Parameter Type Calculation/Input Units Supply rate for given food groups \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
