				\begin{document}$${yb}_{i}= {p}_{i}\div 10000$$\end{document} Fraction The probabilistic approach represents each input variable through statistical distributions from empirical data rather than fixed values [ 32 , 33 ]. Each model iteration randomly samples values from input distributions to solve the system of equations [ 34 ]. This process repeats across iterations, with each representing a plausible scenario based on defined distributions. We performed 10,000 iterations to ensure stable output distributions and capture model estimate variability. For example, assume that the estimated daily intake of fruits in the adult population follows a log-normal distribution with a mean of 100 g/day and a standard deviation of 50 g, as derived from observed data. In a fixed-point framework, the model would assign 100 g/day to all individuals and proceed with exposure calculations using this single value. However, this ignores both variability in intake across individuals and uncertainty in the parameter estimate. Within the probabilistic framework, each Monte Carlo iteration samples a different intake value from the specified distribution, for instance, 65 g/day in one iteration, 118 g/day in another, and 210 g/day in a third, reflecting the right-skewed nature commonly observed in dietary data. The sampled intake is then multiplied by the corresponding nutrient concentration to estimate exposure for that iteration. Over 10,000 iterations, this process generates a full distribution of exposure outcomes rather than a single estimate. The results form an output distribution reflecting uncertainty in the food system. The model uses distributions and iterations to enable meaningful interpretation of likely scenarios and risk ranges in supply and consumption estimates [ 35 ]. Figure 2 shows three core estimation processes: (1) forecasting food supply, (2) estimating dietary consumption, and (3) adjusting intake for age and weekly variability. These estimations were followed by nutrient intake calculation for exposure assessment and dietary adequacy evaluation using the EAR cut-point method [ 26 , 27 ]. Fig. 2 Data sources and computational workflow of the NNC‑V2 Model

sections.food supply modeling:
