sections.modeling dietary adequacy assessment:
Nutrient intake adequacy was evaluated using a Monte Carlo–based cut-point framework previously developed and validated by de Lauzon [ 26 ], which was formally compared with both conventional probability approach and deterministic approaches. Within this framework, nutrient requirements were treated stochastically rather than as fixed cut-points. During each simulation iteration, a requirement value was randomly sampled from a distribution centered on the Average Requirement (AR). The requirement distribution was constructed by applying a coefficient of variation (CV) of 15% to represent inter-individual variability, consistent with the referenced methodology. All value that representing the AR for adults was extracted from by Allen et al. [ 31 ]. It is worth noting that the conventional probability approach typically applies nutrient-specific CVs, with requirement variability defined separately for each nutrient based on available evidence [ 26 , 27 , 31 ]. In contrast, the Monte Carlo–based framework proposed and evaluated by de Lauzon [ 26 ] applies a standardized CV of 15% across nutrients. In their comparative evaluation, this specification produced estimates comparable to those obtained using both the nutrient-specific probability approach and the deterministic cut-point method. Accordingly, a CV of 15% was consistently applied to all nutrients in the present study following the referenced methodology. After generating the probabilistic values for nutrient intake and requirements, the prevalence of inadequacy ( \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
