sections.economic model:
A cohort-based simulation model estimated the excess lifetime burden of four psoriatic disease comorbidities (CVD, MHDs, T2DM and obesity) and potential comorbidity-related gross cost offsets associated with timely systemic treatment with csDMARDs (methotrexate, cyclosporine and acitretin), bDMARDs (TNF- α, IL 12/12, IL 17 and IL 23) or tsDMARDs (apremilast). The analysis used a simple decision tree comparing systemic treatment versus topical/no intervention. This static structure applied treatment-associated relative risk reductions that may translate into lower comorbidity incidence. The analysis extrapolated comorbidity-related costs over a lifetime horizon, without modelling time-dependent transitions, disease progression or treatment discontinuation. Given the simplified structure and absence of longitudinal disease dynamics, the projected differences in comorbidity burden should be interpreted as scenario-based estimates rather than definitive real-world estimates. On the basis of the shared inflammatory pathways affected by systemic treatments, two comparison groups were defined: Topical/no intervention: all patients with moderate-to-severe psoriasis or psoriatic arthritis, untreated or receiving only topical therapy. Systemic treatment: all patients with moderate-to-severe psoriasis or psoriatic arthritis, receiving, and adhering to csDMARDs, bDMARDs or tsDMARDs. This comparison provides a biologically grounded contrast for estimating the comorbidity-related cost offset attributable to systemic therapy. We modelled the treatment effect of csDMARDs, bDMARDs and tsDMARDs using an overall relative risk reduction (RRR) applied at the drug-class level. The csDMARD RRR was based on estimates for methotrexate, cyclosporine and acitretin. The bDMARD RRR was based on biologics targeting TNF-α, IL-12/23, IL-17 and IL-23. The tsDMARD RRR was based on apremilast. These classes include treatments with different mechanisms and effect sizes, so the groups are heterogeneous. We did not model the distribution of treatments within each class and therefore assumed equal weighting across included therapies when estimating the RRR. Some treatment effects were taken directly from the literature, while others were estimated using differences in comorbidity prevalence between treatment groups reported in observational studies. Direct evidence was used for four inputs: The reduction in CVD risk for csDMARDs based on the reported reduction in myocardial infarction associated with bDMARDs versus topical therapies [ 44 ]. The reduction in depression prevalence among patients receiving csDMARDs [ 35 ]. The reduction in depression prevalence among patients receiving bDMARDs [ 35 ]. The proportion of patients achieving ≥ 5% weight loss after initiating tsDMARD (apremilast), which was used as the obesity effect for tsDMARDs [ 36 ]. All other treatment effects were estimated using differences in comorbidity prevalence between treatment groups reported in Darwin et al. (2023) [ 34 ]. The estimation formula is provided below. \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$RR\,(Treatment\,A\,vs\,topical\,treatment)\,=\,\frac{Comorbidity\,prevalence\,under\,Treatment\,A}{Comorbidity\,prevalence\,under\,Treatment\,B}*RR\,(Treatment\,B\,vs\,topical\,treatment)$$\end{document} R R ( T r e a t m e n t A v s t o p i c a l t r e a t m e n t ) = C o m o r b i d i t y p r e v a l e n c e u n d e r T r e a t m e n t A C o m o r b i d i t y p r e v a l e n c e u n d e r T r e a t m e n t B ∗ R R ( T r e a t m e n t B v s t o p i c a l t r e a t m e n t ) Relative risks for treatments without direct evidence were estimated by scaling a known relative risk using the ratio of observed comorbidity prevalence between treatment groups. The T2DM risk reductions for bDMARDs and tsDMARDs were then derived by adjusting the csDMARD estimate according to the differences in T2DM prevalence observed between treatment groups. This approach assumed that treatments associated with lower T2DM prevalence correspond to greater reductions in T2DM risk. The obesity risk reductions for csDMARDs and bDMARDs were derived from the tsDMARD obesity estimate (25%) [ 36 ]. Specifically, the tsDMARD estimate was adjusted using the same proportional differences between treatments observed for T2DM prevalence, assuming that the relative differences between treatments for obesity follow a similar pattern to those observed for T2DM. Finally, the CVD risk reduction for csDMARDs was estimated by combining the bDMARD CVD effect with the difference in CVD prevalence between bDMARD and csDMARD groups, and the tsDMARD CVD effect was derived by adjusting the bDMARD estimate using the difference in myocardial infarction prevalence between tsDMARD and bDMARD groups. This approach allowed treatment effects to be estimated consistently across therapies despite limited direct comparative evidence. This approach enables estimation of treatment effects when direct evidence is lacking by using observational prevalence data. However, differences in prevalence may reflect confounding rather than causal treatment effects, and assuming similar patterns across comorbidities introduces additional uncertainty. The analysis did not incorporate condition-specific prevention of comorbidities, such as statin treatment for CVD, glucose-lowering treatment for diabetes or targeted interventions for obesity and depression. The model therefore focused solely on psoriasis-directed treatment and its associated relative risk reductions. As a result, the estimated comorbidity burden reflects a simplified, psoriasis-centred scenario and may not fully represent real-world risk modification achieved through standard preventive care. By isolating psoriasis treatment-associated risk effects, the analysis aims to clarify the extent to which psoriasis-directed therapy alone may influence projected comorbidity costs. Example populations were defined using prevalence data from Denmark, the USA and Vietnam, selected for data availability and geographic diversity. In Denmark and Vietnam, we estimated the annual direct costs attributable to comorbidities in patients with psoriasis (CVD, T2DM, obesity and MHDs) using a two-step, top-down approach owing to limited country-specific data on comorbidity-attributable costs. First, we used published estimates of the total annual direct cost of psoriasis in each country and inflated these values to 2025 price level. Subsequently, we estimated the proportion of costs attributable to the selected comorbidities on the basis of Feldman et al. (2017), who reported the annual direct costs for patients with psoriasis, with or without comorbidities [ 23 ]. We estimated that, on average, patients with psoriasis and comorbidities have 33% higher annual costs. This proportion was applied to the Danish and Vietnamese total psoriasis-related costs to estimate the direct costs attributable to the comorbidities in Denmark and Vietnam. Lastly, we distributed this total comorbidity-related cost across individual conditions using the relative incremental cost contribution of each comorbidity reported in the same study. This method provides pragmatic and internally consistent estimates but assumes that USA-derived comorbidity cost distributions are transferable to Denmark and Vietnam; therefore, the results should be interpreted as modelled projections rather than empirically observed comorbidity-attributable costs. In the absence of country-specific comorbidity risk estimates for Denmark and Vietnam, baseline prevalence inputs were sourced from Germany and China, respectively. China was selected as a comparator for Vietnam primarily owing to geographic proximity. Germany was selected as a comparator for Denmark because both are high-income European countries. These proxy risk estimates were applied without country-specific recalibration. Although this approach enabled model parametrisation in the absence of local data, differences in epidemiology, healthcare utilisation and diagnostic practices may limit direct comparability; therefore, the results should be interpreted with appropriate caution. A lifetime horizon captured comorbidity-related costs over remaining life expectancy, assuming equal survival across treatment groups. Outputs included total and per-capita lifetime costs for each treatment group, and direct and indirect gross cost offsets by treatment type. Model assumptions and inputs were informed by the literature review and are detailed in the Results section.

sections.introduction:
Developing and understanding biomarkers of schizophrenia (SZ) is a critical endeavor: such physiology-based features could provide knowledge on potential diagnostic features, future pharmaceutical targets or early indicators of disease progression and therapy response [ 1 – 3 ]. Functional neuroimaging markers – especially those obtained via electroencephalography (EEG) [ 4 , 5 ] – appear as an intuitive choice for this purpose [ 6 ], with resting-state EEG providing a fast and convenient way to infer on brain dynamics. Currently, the most consistent resting-state EEG alterations identified in SZ include an increase in delta- and theta-band activity, while a decrease in alpha power [ 7 , 8 ], though contradictory findings are also reported [ 9 ]. Furthermore, extensive dynamic functional connectivity literature indicates that short-scale neural dynamics are altered in SZ, often in a way that is undetectable for conventional, time-invariant analytical approaches [ 10 , 11 ]. Interestingly, however, despite all this evidence, the temporal dynamics of resting-state EEG power spectral features are rarely considered in SZ. Altered cortical excitation/inhibition (E/I) ratio is a pathological feature that is considered central in SZ [ 12 ] with explicit potential for clinical utility [ 13 , 14 ]. The 1/ f slope ( \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
