sections.adhesion assays:
The adhesion of each S. aureus isolate to human Fg and Fn was assessed using a solid-phase whole-cell ligand binding assay. Bacteria were grown to mid-log phase (OD₆₀₀ ≈ 0.5, Fn binding) or stationary phase (18 h culture, Fg binding), normalized to OD₆₀₀ of 1 in PBS, and added to 384-well Nunclon plates pre-coated with purified human Fg (0–10 µg/mL) or Fn (0–20 µg/mL). Wells were blocked with 5% (v/v) BSA in PBS prior to bacterial addition. After 1 h incubation, non-adherent cells were removed by vigorous washing. The attached bacteria were fixed, stained with crystal violet, and the dye was solubilized with acetic acid. Absorbance at 570 nm was measured to quantify adherent biomass. Each isolate was evaluated in at least three independent experiments, with four technical replicates per condition. Adhesion levels are presented as background-subtracted absorbance values, normalized to the adhesion of the SH100 strain under the specified conditions. Whole-cell adhesion was measured across a range of ligand concentrations (0–10 μg/mL Fg and 0–20 μg/mL Fn). For association analyses, we focused on adhesion at a low coating concentration (Fg 1.25 and Fn 5 μg/mL), high concentration (Fg 5 and Fn 20 μg/mL) and on the adhesion curve slope (D-value). These two concentrations and parameter were chosen as they yielded the broadest dynamic range among isolates. By modeling cell adhesion to a ligand as a second-order binding reaction 83 (see Supplementary Method 1 ), we conclude that the initial slope of the dose–response absorbance curve at low ligand concentrations, D =dA(c = 0)/dc, reflects the ligand-binding capacity of each S. aureus isolate. Specifically, D is related to the ligand-bacterium dissociation constant \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
